Package: mysteryroster
Title: Registry Overlap Strata and Listing-Validity Analysis for Physician Directory Audits
Version: 0.3.0
Authors@R:
    person("Tyler", "Muffly", email = "tyler.muffly@dhha.org", role = c("aut", "cre"),
           comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-2044-1693"))
Description: Tools for studies that test whether physician listings in national
    directories are valid. Cross-links any number of registries (for example NPPES,
    a certifying board, a professional society) by a common identifier into mutually
    exclusive overlap zones, draws a stratified listing sample that preserves sampling
    probabilities, turns telephone-audit dispositions into four exhaustive listing
    statuses (confirmed valid, confirmed invalid, unresolved, not attempted), and
    produces design-weighted validity estimates, extreme-case sensitivity bounds,
    survey-weighted regression, and study-flow counts. Also compares a shared
    attribute (such as practice state) pairwise across sources with no gold
    standard, freshness-tuned by each source's own last-updated or vintage date,
    including a set-based comparison for attributes a source may legitimately
    report more than one value for; offers three longitudinal lead-lag approaches
    (panel convergence, spell-age stratified agreement, and administrative
    enrollment timing) for determining which of two equally fallible sources is
    leading versus lagging; extends to three or more sources at once (unanimous,
    majority, or genuine split; a lone dissenter; which source the group converges
    toward); and fuses declared same-lineage sources into one predicted value by
    majority vote, with explicit abstention on ties or weak majorities. A separate
    sub-workflow runs an HHS Office of Inspector General-style directory audit
    protocol, keeping completeness, consistency, and independently verified
    accuracy as three distinct populations rather than one conflated denominator:
    a field dictionary with declared source lineage, population separation,
    four-way field-presence classification, enrollment-to-location linkage that
    never asserts clinical presence, and a frozen per-claim verification
    questionnaire reconciled against structured nonresponse. Specialty-
    agnostic: the ENT registry-validity study and the OB/GYN subspecialty study
    use the same functions with different registries and disposition codebooks.
